a top commander with the islamic jihad  militant group in gaza has reportedly  been killed in an israeli airstrike  the death of hussam abu harbid comes as  the conflict between israel  and islamist militants rages in the  enclave  the israeli army said harbid was behind  several anti-tank missile terror attacks  against israeli civilians  this included one attack on the first  day of the current round of fighting  that the army said had injured a  civilian in israel prime minister  benjamin netanyahu said  that attacks in gaza are continuing at  full force  and will take time  the aftermath of israel's latest  airstrikes on  gaza residents are left picking through  the rubble that used to be their homes  they say the attacks were the most  ferocious yet  we were certainly scared especially the  children  we feared they would be wounded that  they would die or be buried under the  rubble  israel says it's aiming to destroy  terrorist infrastructure  and is targeting militants the army  later released footage  it says shows strikes killing a senior  palestinian militant commander  israel's jets also targeted an  underground network of tunnels  used by the militants in their early  morning raid  a residential complex in the city of  ashdod hit by a hamas rocket  hamas has kept up its barrage in  southern israel  not all missiles are intercepted by  israel's defenses  as the bombs keep falling the  international community is becoming  increasingly alarmed  denmark the us says it's working to  defuse the situation  the united states remains greatly  concerned  by by the violence by the escalating  violence  hundreds of people killed or injured  including  children being pulled from the rubble  we'll continue to conduct intensive  diplomacy  to bring this current cycle of violence  to an end but as the killing continues  violence fear and loss remain an  everyday experience  for these children in gaza  dw's tanya kramer is covering this story  for you and she joins us now from  jerusalem uh tanya we understand that  gaza has come under heavy bombardment  again what's the latest information you  have for us from the enclave  well on the one hand as we speak that we  had some rocket alerts so incoming fire  from the gaza strip towards southern  communities and cities  in israel but they have also been again  a very heavy night of bombardments in  the gaza strip  and during the day airstrikes i just  talked to some people there  on the phone what is also very  concerning there is now the humanitarian  situation it is a  sealed off enclave for many many years  there are a lot of shortages  anyway but people are saying they're  having now only about three hours of  electricity  um power lines where apparently are not  working out properly  they haven't worked before either but  now they're cut down  again and that also affects uh you know  water and sewage  and and everything else and also there  has been no humanitarian corridor now  this  is the seventh or eighth day soon  of this campaign one week and they need  new supplies to come in as well from the  humanitarian side  uh tanya uh israeli prime minister mr  benjamin netanyahu said yesterday that  the conflict would take  time and will continue at full force  is there the political will to look for  a way out  well at least in the public safe  statements we're hearing uh that the  israeli government and benjamin  netanyahu the prime minister has said  that very clearly again yesterday  they feel they still have the support of  many countries  international communities especially  also from the us to continue the  military campaign  we could assume that also there are  domestic considerations  uh in this equation there's a lot of  pressure here to achieve something with  this campaign not just to  to stop in the middle as some people  here have told me  um to have a decisive victory so to  speak  and to end it once and for all it's not  quite clear what  is meant by this and also the military  has said they want to continue  as long as they can continue to degrade  all the military  military capabilities of hamas and  islamic jihad  now tanya in conclusion calls for  ceasefire  are growing what can you tell us about  any diplomatic efforts that are  underway well it's very hard you know to  get  any solid information at the moment  there are a lot of reports we know the  united nations special envoy in  jerusalem  is in talks that is also the traditional  role  here but also first and foremost egypt  is  usually the mediator here between hamas  and israel  they're well-established channels they  have done this before  and we are expecting them also to  mediate  in this regard in this matter again and  also the us  envoy that was sent here hadi ammar is  also still on the ground so there are a  lot of  different efforts but it's not so  tangible at the moment  when there would be a breakthrough dwsa  tanya akramer reporting tanya thank you  for your  tireless coverage i greatly appreciate  it  i'm joined now by jeremy isajadov he's  israel ambassador  to germany mr ambassador thank you so  much for coming in today  yesterday was one of the deadliest days  since the fighting began what needs to  happen for the bloodshed to end  well basically what should have happened  in the beginning the hamas should not  have attacked israel with now  over 3 000 rockets and this started last  monday with a salvo against jerusalem  and has continued throughout the week  and this has been one of the most  intense attacks on israeli population  centers that we've had  even though they've been previous  exchanges  and so bottom line is what needs to  happen is that this  has to stop and we are doing all in our  power to protect our citizens  to intercept these rockets and to ensure  that these rockets are not only fired  today but also in the coming  months and years now especially in the  past week what has shocked the world  was the civilian casualties in gaza how  would you respond to people calling  israel's operation disproportionate well  first of all i think as i said  that the attack by hamas on israel with  such  a heavy number of rockets and missiles  and again  let's not forget these are embedded in  the palestinian  infrastructure and they are targeting  israeli civilians and cities  i want to also emphasize one thing  virtually in our estimation 20 to 30  percent of the rockets  also fall within gaza and they kill  palestinians  so i think that when people talk about  the situation in gaza first and foremost  they should come to hamas  and say why did you start this horrific  exchange who needed it what are you  trying to achieve is hamas trying  the damage is done isn't it now now the  damage is done  and has to be prevented again what was  hamas trying to do to make peace with  israel  to bring israel into a peace process  which they don't believe in and which  they're trying to undermine  uh to help the palestinian authority who  they're trying also to undermine  it just did not you know have any sense  so i mean  when you ask me today as an israeli  diplomat what is the logic of  of you know this carrying on i asked  what is the logic of it happening in the  first place  and there one must really come in full  force to hamas  and have them explain themselves in that  sense  a couple of days ago the israeli  military attacked a building that  housed um some international media  houses um  al-jazeera and ap i'm going to  quote um an ap statement saying the  world will know  less about what is happening in gaza  because of what happened there  was the real purpose of this airstrike  to prevent international reporting on  what is happening  no to the best of my knowledge no and  everything i can see on television  whether it's on  cnl al jazeera or the other stations on  deutsche weller  i don't think there's any lack of  coverage and we wouldn't do that in  order to stop  coverage we had very clear information  about  the facilities that would have been  serving  hamas in terms of their intelligence  capabilities their cyber  capabilities and also very particular  equipment  and i'm pretty sure that not everyone  realizes the extent to which hamas again  embeds itself  in the civilian population in order to  try and have a human shield so if anyone  should come to  any uh uh complaints here it should be  to hamas  that that what they did was no uh  less of a war crime than fighting and  shooting against israeli civilians  uh in israel now with all of this the  international community is asking for a  ceasefire but prime minister netanyahu  says the fighting will continue until  calm is restored  how far is israel willing to go look  we didn't seek this exchange and  essentially i think you need again  hamas needs to cease the rocket fire on  israel  and until it does israel will not this  is the end defending its citizens  so i mean the question has to be  addressed to them  uh and as far as what i'm concerned i do  not  see this as something that has to be  protracted without end  uh eventually it'll come to a close  there are a lot of different  conversations happening now  what are the conversations there are  conversations between the americans and  israel  we also have very open channels between  the foreign minister of germany who's  speaking to qatar  speaking to egypt and he's also speaking  frequently to  gabi ashkenazi the foreign minister and  i also believe they'll also be very uh  continuing high-level discussions that  will go on in the  coming hours all right we've seen a  flare-up in sectarian  violence in israel over the  past couple of days how worried are you  about the growing rift in your country  first of all i i i i'm worried because i  saw  actually in the last months a growing  desire by the israeli arab community to  be more and more involved in israeli  politics  and you could see this in a range of  things that happened after the last  election and possibilities of coalition  i thought this was an incredibly  important development i think hamas is  very scared of  any type of israeli jewish  arab conciliation so this is a byproduct  of the exchange  which has caused riots in mixed cities  which up till now been a very  major example of coexistence so  i am concerned about it but i believe  living in jerusalem and having  many palestinian and israeli arab  friends that  the ones who are interested in quiet  which are the vast majority of jews and  arabs will want to reassert their right  to live  in peace and in coexistence so  ultimately i  i think that that is what will prevail  right let's talk about the situation  here in germany over the weekend we've  seen  a series of uh pro-palestinian  demonstrations  some of them um saw altercations and  were overshadowed by  anti-semitic rhetoric is the german  government doing enough to curb this  kind of behavior well i think first of  all uh i've spoken  to the some of the initial incidents of  concern where in  north rhine with westphalia i spoke to  amin lashed  the prime minister of the of the state  and he was  very concerned about it and we discussed  and he assured me that they will do all  in their power to prevent it  other senior security officials uh spoke  to me  and also assured me that they will do  all in their power look some of these  uh things of uh fighting against uh you  know jewish communities confronting them  attacking synagogues burning israeli  flags uh these are things  you know it's one thing to have a  peaceful demonstration but another thing  to have these type of acts  which really i mean the jewish community  in germany has  nothing to do with the crisis going on  in gaza and  they are deserving of living like any  other  community in germany in peace and  security and i  um based on what i have heard also from  president steinmeier who spoke out very  loudly against these  attacks also from what i've heard from  horse seahoffer the minister of interior  who came out against these attacks and  he said we will use  all in our power to stop these type of  anti-semitic attacks  so i uh from that point of view i feel  the german authorities will do all they  can to prevent  are you currently concerned about the  security of israelis and jews here in  germany  look i'm always concerned and as i said  i think that these communities should  live quietly and in peace and i must say  i don't want to paint the picture  just negatively we've also had  demonstrations of support and solidarity  from israel  not only from the you know all works of  life in german  in germany but also from even um local  arab leader  arab muslim leaders um and i think  that's important to remember they've  been  support here in leipzig in frankfurt and  even in berlin last friday there was a  meeting with the minister of justice  christina lambrecht with the  minister of culture monica grutas and  also the head of the green party  annalina bierbook  that came to express their support and  to very  much negate any form of anti-semitism  against jews in germany and i and i  think that that is also a very strong  uh sense of uh uh sentiment that i  i feel very uh um at least reassured by  ambassador jeremy aissa thank you very  much for your time today thank you for  having me

